## Theoretical Complexity Recap

| Operation | `std::vector` | `std::list` (doubly-linked) |
|---|---|---|
| Insert at position *i* | O(n) amortized* | O(1)*† |
| Traverse/access element *i* | O(1) random access | O(i) sequential walk |
| Memory overhead per element | ~0 bytes extra | ~16–24 bytes (prev/next pointers) |
| Cache friendliness | Excellent | Poor |

\* Assuming iterator is already positioned at the insertion point.  
† For `std::list`, finding the position is still O(i).

> **Critical insight**: Both containers require O(i) time to *locate* the insertion point if you don't already hold an iterator there. The "O(1)" claim for `std::list` only applies when you **already have an iterator** to the insertion site.

---

## Why Cache Locality Matters So Much

### `std::vector`
- Elements are stored in a **contiguous block of memory**.
- Modern CPUs prefetch data aggressively; sequential or near-sequential access patterns hit L1/L2 cache frequently.
- Even with memmove overhead, operating on cache-resident data is extremely fast.

### `std::list`
- Each node is individually heap-allocated.
- Nodes are scattered across the heap → **poor spatial locality**.
- Every pointer chase (`node->next`) risks a **cache miss**, which costs ~50–300 cycles vs ~1 cycle for a cache hit.
- Additionally, each allocation/deallocation has overhead from the allocator and potential fragmentation.

---

