The classic O(n) vs O(1) comparison ignores critical real-world factors:

| Factor | `std::vector` | `std::list` |
|---|---|---|
| **Cache locality** | Excellent (contiguous memory) | Poor (pointer-chasing, scattered nodes) |
| **Memory allocation** | Amortized few allocs; bulk allocation | One allocation per node |
| **Pointer indirection** | None | Two pointers per node + data access via deref |
| **Branch prediction** | Predictable linear scan | Unpredictable pointer chains |
| **Memory overhead** | 0 bytes per element | ~16–24 bytes per node (prev/next pointers) |
| **Insertion cost breakdown** | Memmove dominates | Allocation + pointer updates dominate |

