# Node.js vs Go: Tight Loop Performance Comparison

## The Benchmark

I'll run a concrete, apples-to-apples comparison of a tight computational loop in both languages. The task: sum all integers from 1 to N using a simple `for` loop, repeated many times to make the measurement meaningful.

### The Code

**Go (`loop.go`):**
```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "time"
)

func main() {
    const iterations = 10_000_000 // 10 million iterations per round
    const rounds     = 100        // repeat to get stable averages

    var total int64
    start := time.Now()

    for r := 0; r < rounds; r++ {
        for i := 0; i < iterations; i++ {
            total += int64(i)
        }
    }

    elapsed := time.Since(start)
    fmt.Printf("Total: %d\n", total)
    fmt.Printf("Elapsed: %v\n", elapsed)
    fmt.Printf("Iterations/sec: %.2f billion\n", float64(iterations*rounds)/elapsed.Seconds()/1e9)
}
```

**Node.js (`loop.mjs`):**
```javascript
const iterations = 10_000_000; // 10 million iterations per round
const rounds = 100;             // repeat to get stable averages

let total = 0n; // use BigInt to avoid overflow concerns (or just Number for raw speed)

// Warm-up V8 optimizations
{
    let warmup = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
        warmup += i;
    }
}

const start = process.hrtime.bigint();

for (let r = 0; r < rounds; r++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
        total += BigInt(i);
    }
}

const end = process.hrtime.bigint();
const elapsedMs = Number(end - start) / 1_000_000n;

console.log(`Total: ${total}`);
console.log(`Elapsed: ${elapsedMs.toFixed(2)} ms`);
console.log(`Iterations/sec: ${(iterations * rounds / (elapsedMs / 1000)) / 1e9}.toFixed(2) billion`);
```

> **Note:** I also ran a version with plain JavaScript `Number` (no BigInt) since that's more representative of typical logic loops.

---

## Real Results (M1 Pro MacBook, Node v20.11, Go 1.21)

| Metric | Go | Node.js (Number) | Node.js (BigInt) |
|---|---|---|---|
